---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import Closing from '../components/Closing.astro';
import { product } from '../data/product';
const manifest =
  '{\n  "apiVersion": 1,\n  "id": "com.example.workspace-note",\n  "name": "Workspace Note",\n  "version": "1.0.0",\n  "permissions": ["workspace:read", "files:read"],\n  "contributes": {\n    "workspaceTools": [{\n      "id": "note",\n      "title": "Note",\n      "icon": "book",\n      "entry": "index.html"\n    }]\n  }\n}';
---

<Base
  title="Make it yours"
  description="Extend the mxwl editor with standalone workspace plugins. Build tools with HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and a versioned, permission-gated SDK."
>
  <header class="page-hero container">
    <div class="eyebrow">AN EDITOR WITH AN OPEN DOOR</div>
    <h1>
      Your workflow
      <br />
      gets a seat here<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A task board. A branch artifact reader. A view into your internal tools. Build the surface
      your work needs, then put it right next to the code.
    </p>
    <div class="button-row">
      <a class="button primary" href="/docs/plugins/">
        Build your first plugin
      </a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/docs/plugin-api/">
        Explore the SDK
      </a>
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="container detail-grid">
    <div>
      <div class="eyebrow">ORDINARY WEB TOOLS / YOUR OWN DIRECTORY</div>
      <h2>
        A manifest.
        <br />A page.
        <br />A place in the deck.
      </h2>
      <p>
        Plugins are standalone folders of HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and a <code>mxwl.plugin.json</code>{' '}
        manifest. You can keep one in its own repository, separate from the editor.
      </p>
      <p>
        Paste the folder or manifest path into <strong>Settings → Plugins</strong>, review its
        permissions, and enable it. mxwl links it in place. Reload picks up your edits; unlinking
        leaves your source intact.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${product.repository}/tree/main/examples/plugins/task-board`}>
        Start with the Task Board example
      </a>
    </div>
    <CodeBlock code={manifest} label="mxwl.plugin.json" />
  </section>
  <section class="container page-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">THE WORKSPACE IS THE API</div>
        <h2>Context, without the glue.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        The versioned browser SDK gives a plugin the active workspace context and access to
        capabilities approved by the user.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-index">
      <div class="index-item">
        <div class="index-top">
          <Icon name="folder" size={26} />
          <span class="number">[01]</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Follow the workspace.</h3>
        <p>
          React to branch, Git state, connection, and workspace changes. Your tool knows where the
          work is happening.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="index-item">
        <div class="index-top">
          <Icon name="code" size={26} />
          <span class="number">[02]</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Use the host’s capabilities.</h3>
        <p>
          Read files, inspect Git, interact with browser tabs, prompt an agent, or use scoped
          storage and HTTP—with the relevant permissions.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="index-item">
        <div class="index-top">
          <Icon name="puzzle" size={26} />
          <span class="number">[03]</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Live inside the editor.</h3>
        <p>
          Contribute a workspace tool to the same deck used by the built-in Code Explorer and
          Changes plugins.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="statement">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">A USEFUL EXAMPLE</div>
        <h2>
          Turn branch artifacts
          <br />
          into a living brief.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Read the spec, plan, QA evidence, and review notes associated with the active branch.
          Offer heading navigation and search, then refresh as artifacts change.
        </p>
        <p>Your team’s conventions live in the plugin, and the editor stays general.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/plugin-recipes/">
          Explore plugin recipes
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container page-section">
    <div class="detail-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">EXPLICIT CAPABILITIES</div>
        <h2>
          Small surface.
          <br />
          Clear permissions.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Linked plugins run in sandboxed iframes with their own origins and a restrictive Content
          Security Policy. Host operations go through the SDK and are checked on every request.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Plugins start disabled. Permission changes require a fresh review before they can run
          again. Request only the capabilities your tool uses.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/plugin-security/">
            The security model
          </a>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/plugin-testing/">
            Testing & distribution
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Closing />
</Base>
